"In Praise of Shadows (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In_Praise_of_Shadows)\nciteturn36search12 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; Publisher: Various ... Tanizaki's observations include cultural notes on customs and tradition, people, historical places and buildings, discussion of various materials and craft techniques, as well as food and even unusual recipes as seen through the author's metaphorical lens of light and shadow.The original essay was published in Japan in 1933. ... Interview: Puma Blue and the Poignant Poetry of Debut Album 'In Praise of Shadows'. ... The West, in its striving for progress, is presented as continuously searching for light and clarity, while the subtle and subdued forms of East Asian art and literature are seen by Tanizaki to represent an appreciation of shadow and subtlety, closely relating to the traditional Japanese concept of sabi.\nIn Praise of Shadows\n1933 essay by Jun'ichirō Tanizaki\n\nName: In Praise of Shadows\nCaption: English language cover\nTitle Orig: (In'ei Raisan)\nOrig Lang Code: ja\nAuthor: Jun'ichirō Tanizaki\nCountry: Japan\nLanguage: Japanese\nGenre: Philosophy, aesthetics\nPublisher: Various\nPub Date: 1933 (original)\nPages: 59 (original essay)\n\nis an essay by Jun'ichirō Tanizaki about Japanese aesthetics. Tanizaki's observations include cultural notes on customs and tradition, people, historical places and buildings, discussion of various materials and craft techniques, as well as food and even unusual recipes as seen through the author's metaphorical lens of light and shadow. The original essay was published in Japan in 1933.\n\nThe 1977 English translation includes a foreword by the architect Charles Moore, who writes that the book \"helps us to look deep into ourselves to our own inhabitation of our world, as it describes with spine-tingling insights the traditional Japanese inhabitation of theirs. In Praise of Shadows\n## Publication history\n\nTanizaki's aesthetic credo was already sketched out in a series of comments that appear in his 1929 novel Some Prefer Nettles, and subsequently published in the more polished form of in 1933. Much shorter than the author's novels, this book is a small meditative work of 73 pages, of which 59 are the essay itself.[citation needed]\n\n### English translations\n\nThe first English translation was published in 1977 by Leete's Island Books. This edition contains a foreword by the architect Charles Moore, and an afterword by Thomas J. Harper, one of the translators. Harper was Senior Lecturer in Japanese Literature at the Australian National University in Canberra. The other translator, Edward Seidensticker, was a Professor of Japanese Literature at Columbia University. Seidensticker's students assisted with the translation. Penguin Random House has released various paperback reprints and ebook formats of this version (Jonathan Cape, Vintage Classics, Vintage, etc.).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEditions for Éloge de l'ombre | The StoryGraph (https://app.thestorygraph.com/books/2c4b6ad1-86c7-43b0-b207-0029602ec5a4/editions)\nciteturn36search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Original Pub Year: 1933 ... ### In Praise of Shadows ... Publisher: Leetes Island Books\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows 陰翳礼賛（英文版） | チャールズ・イー・タトル出版 (https://www.tuttle.co.jp/products/show/isbn%3A9784805306659)\nciteturn36search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Image: In Praise of Shadows ... 1933-34年に世に出された本書は、日本人がまだ薄暗い中で生活していた頃の日本家屋の素晴らしさと、その暗がりで生まれる美の数々を賞賛します。\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows? | tuttle publishing (https://www.tuttle.co.jp/products/show/isbn%3A9784805318041/language%3Aen)\nciteturn36search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; This eloquent work on the Japanese sense of beauty explores the subtle interplay of shade and light in several important aspects of Japanese life―architecture, drama, food, femininity, and literature―and traces the retreat of this mature, shadowy aesthetic tradition before the brighter, more garish products of Western technology. ... Originally published in 1933 and 1934<, i> In Praise of Shadows presents readers with Tanizaki's obvious love for the traditions for which his country is renowned and provided a prescient warning of the dangers those traditions face.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEditions for In Praise of Shadows | The StoryGraph (https://app.thestorygraph.com/books/8e4feaaf-5f2c-4390-875f-19bf54af161e/editions)\nciteturn36search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; In Praise of Shadows ... Original Pub Year: 1933 ... Publisher: Leetes Island Books\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows ::: Open WIKI (https://www.owiki.org/wiki/In_Praise_of_Shadows)\nciteturn36search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; In Praise of Shadows is a 1933 essay on Japanese aesthetics by the Japanese author Jun'ichirō Tanizaki.It was translated into English, in 1977, by the academic students of Japanese literature Thomas J.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows (https://wiki.bitter.house/content/wikipedia_en_all_maxi_2024-01/A/In_Praise_of_Shadows)\nciteturn36search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 93.9 years ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; Original title  | 陰翳礼讃 (In'ei Raisan) ... In Praise of Shadows (陰翳礼讃, In'ei Raisan) is a 1933 essay on Japanese aesthetics by the Japanese author Jun'ichirō Tanizaki.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows @ The Open Buddhist University (https://buddhistuniversity.net/content/essays/praise-of-shadows_tanizaki)\nciteturn36search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 93.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; # In Praise 