ncluding automated delays and reverb, sampling and remixing. ... Two years after the release of In Praise of Shadows, Le Boeuf Brothers delivered an album called Remixed that not only made electronica remixes of most of that album's tracks, but included additional artists such as Kissy Girls, Dave Binney, Tim Lefebvre, and others as well.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRemy Le Boeuf (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remy_Le_Boeuf)\nciteturn36search23 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; 2019 | Remy Le Boeuf | Light as a Word | Outside in Music ... 2011 | Le Boeuf Brothers | In Praise of Shadows | Nineteen-Eight Records\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn Praise of Shadows (new translation) (https://www.reddit.com/r/TrueLit/comments/1ur7zub/in_praise_of_shadows_new_translation/)\nciteturn36reddit24 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Has anyone else read Tanizaki's In Praise of Shadows? ... I don't know how well this quality holds up in translation, but from everything I've seen, Edward Seidensticker is an *excellent* translator, so knowing only what I know now, if I wanted to read this work in English, I'd be inclined to go with the original translation (I would probably search out a comparison to the new translations though; I've never read anything by Starr or Cronin, but I'm sure the publishers had their reasons for ordering new translations). ... Tanizaki was living *in* this period, but our understanding of historical processes often becomes much sharper in retrospect, and there's academic work on this era that has a comprehensive viewpoint which just wasn't accessible to Tanizaki in 1933. ... So I'm certainly not trying to make the case that *Shadows* isn't a beautiful work of literature (it is), or isn't fascinating when considered as a historical document (it is).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIn praise of shadows (https://www.reddit.com/r/Supernote/comments/m9dz4a)\nciteturn36reddit25 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.6 years ago; Crawled: 5.6 years ago; For a nice little summary of some of Tanizaki’s thoughts, see this article: ‘In Praise of Shadows: Ancient Japanese Aesthetics and Why Every Technology Is a Technology of Thought‘ URL\n"
